upx_packed_pe_x86_64_lzma.exe -- the MinGW win64/pe fixture for
`kuna_analysis::upx::pe`: every rebuild a PE32+ console program needs.

What it is
  A small MinGW-w64 (llvm-mingw) console program packed with `upx --lzma`
  (UPX 5.2.1). 11,776 bytes packed; 13,824 bytes unpacked. PackHeader at 0x1fd:
  version=13, format=36 (win64/pe), method=14 (LZMA), filter=0x49.

  It carries what the minimal #1294 witness cannot:
    - 39 imports over 11 DLLs, by name and by ordinal (lib.dll #2), including a
      KERNEL32 import by ordinal (#100) -- which UPX records as the offset of its
      own loader thunk rather than as the ordinal -- and import names that were
      never one contiguous run (UPX then leaves them in place);
    - 36 kept DIR64 base relocations (ASLR image), rebuilt from UPX's delta
      stream;
    - a resource tree (icon, icon group, string table, version info) whose
      uncompressed leaves UPX moved out of the image;
    - a TLS directory with a callback, and an exception directory.

  `kuna unpack` writes what `upx -d` (5.3.0) writes except the IAT data
  directory and each import descriptor's OriginalFirstThunk, which upx -d
  zeroes. Against the linker's original, the sections, imports (as a set),
  relocations, TLS and resource leaves all match; .text padding past the virtual
  size, the TLS index slot UPX zeroes, the import descriptor order (UPX sorts the
  DLLs) and the resource directory layout (UPX rebuilds it) differ, exactly as
  in upx -d's output.

Where it came from
  Built in the mstorsjo/llvm-mingw docker image (clang 23.1.4) from three small
  sources: cons.c (printf/malloc/MessageBoxA, a pointer table, a _Thread_local
  counter and a TLS callback, calls into lib.dll by name and by NONAME ordinal,
  and into KERNEL32 by ordinal 100 through a dlltool import library), lib.c
  (see upx_packed_pe_x86_64.dll.provenance), and res.rc (icon, string table,
  version info):

      x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang -O1 -s -shared -o lib.dll lib.c lib.def -Wl,--out-implib,liblib.a
      x86_64-w64-mingw32-dlltool -d k32ord.def -l libk32ord.a
      x86_64-w64-mingw32-windres res.rc -O coff -o res.o
      x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang -O1 -s -o cons.exe cons.c res.o -L. -llib -lk32ord -luser32
      upx --lzma -o upx_packed_pe_x86_64_lzma.exe cons.exe   # UPX 5.2.1

  sha256(original cons.exe)  61cba978266556bacea479b67a3ca78720e337033c2e1d4e24523464679e85d8
  sha256(packed)             b939625e94786b269ccf72952422a0645c32e39b3cf6408aade18f5da8d16bbb

Why it is vendored rather than built
  `upx` is not installed on CI, and neither is a MinGW toolchain.
